namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page maths_spatial_page Spatial data structures

  \tableofcontents

  \section maths_spatial_kdtree Kd-trees

  A kd-tree recursively partitions the space with axis-aligned hyperplanes. In %Ponca (Ponca::KdTreeBase::build),
  each node computes the axis-aligned bounding box of its points, and splits it at its center, along its largest
  dimension: the points whose coordinate is smaller than the center go to the first child, the others to the second
  child. A node becomes a leaf when it contains at most `minCellSize()` points, or at the maximal depth.

  \snippet ponca_numpy.py kdtree

  Splitting at the middle of the box (rather than at the median of the points) bounds the aspect ratio of the cells:
  every split halves the largest side of the box. The tree is not balanced on non-uniform data, but its depth grows
  with the logarithm of the ratio between the extent of the data and the distance between the points; it is bounded
  by `Traits::MAX_DEPTH`. The construction costs \f$O(n)\f$ per level, i.e. \f$O(n \log n)\f$ for well distributed
  data.

  \subsection maths_spatial_queries Queries

  The queries traverse the tree depth-first with a stack (Ponca::KdTreeQuery). At an inner node, the child containing
  the query position is visited first; the other child is only visited if the squared distance between the query and
  the splitting hyperplane, \f$(q_k - s)^2\f$ for a split at \f$x_k = s\f$, is smaller than the squared search radius.
  The search radius is fixed for range queries, and shrinks for nearest and k-nearest neighbor queries, as it is the
  distance to the farthest of the best candidates found so far (stored in a Ponca::LimitedPriorityQueue). The
  pruning test only uses the last splitting plane, a lower bound of the distance to the cell: a few cells that do not
  intersect the ball may be visited (Figure 1). For a range query returning \f$k\f$ points, the cost is typically
  \f$O(\log n + k)\f$ on well-distributed data.

  \image html maths/maths_kdtree.png "Figure 1. Bounding boxes of the leaves of a 2D kd-tree built with the splitting rule of Ponca (here with at most 12 points per leaf, 64 by default), on uniform points plus a dense cluster. The leaves visited by a range query are filled: they include all the leaves intersecting the ball, and a few others that the pruning test cannot discard."

  \section maths_spatial_graphs Neighbor graphs

  A k-nearest neighbor graph (Ponca::KnnGraph) stores, for each point, its \f$k\f$ nearest neighbors, computed once
  with \f$n\f$ k-nearest neighbor queries in the kd-tree. A neighbor graph (Ponca::NeighborGraph) stores, for each
  point, all the points closer than a distance \f$\varepsilon\f$ (an \f$\varepsilon\f$-graph). Querying the neighbors
  of a point then amounts to reading a range of indices.

  The range queries of the graphs explore the graph from the query point, and only cross edges whose end point lies in
  the ball. They therefore return the points of the ball connected to the query by paths of points in the ball: a
  subset of the result of the kd-tree range query, which follows the connectivity of the sampled surface. With a k-NN
  graph, this subset is complete when \f$k\f$ is large enough with respect to the sampling density in the ball; when
  the ball contains several sheets of the surface that the graph does not connect, only the sheet containing the query
  is returned.
 */
} // namespace Ponca
